1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,586
Welcome to lesson thirteen: autoscaling.

2
00:00:03,686 --> 00:00:13,728
You'll make the cluster grow when load rises and shrink when it falls: more Pods, bigger Pods, and more nodes. That way you handle traffic spikes without paying for idle capacity.

3
00:00:15,078 --> 00:00:17,424
Autoscaling works in layers.

4
00:00:17,524 --> 00:00:31,262
The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes the number of Pods. KEDA does the same based on events, and can scale to zero. The Vertical Pod Autoscaler changes the size of Pods. And Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter change the number of nodes.

5
00:00:31,362 --> 00:00:39,564
They work as a chain. Traffic rises, the HPA adds Pods, some can't fit and stay Pending, and the node autoscaler adds a node.

6
00:00:39,664 --> 00:00:45,162
All of them depend on resource requests. Without good requests, autoscaling doesn't work.

7
00:00:46,512 --> 00:00:48,554
The HPA needs metrics.

8
00:00:48,654 --> 00:00:57,688
metrics-server provides CPU and memory. Adapters like the Prometheus Adapter provide your app's own metrics, and external metrics such as queue length.

9
00:00:57,788 --> 00:01:04,454
If kubectl top says the metrics API isn't available, every CPU-based autoscaler will show unknown.

10
00:01:05,804 --> 00:01:14,230
Here's an HPA. It targets a Deployment, keeps between three and thirty replicas, and aims for sixty percent CPU utilization.

11
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:18,292
Utilization is measured as a percentage of the CPU request.

12
00:01:18,392 --> 00:01:29,538
Every fifteen seconds it calculates: current replicas, times the current value, divided by the target, rounded up. Four Pods at ninety percent with a sixty percent target gives six.

13
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:36,306
A few details. Within ten percent of the target, nothing changes, which avoids flapping.

14
00:01:36,406 --> 00:01:39,680
With several metrics, the highest recommendation wins.

15
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:50,094
Scaling up happens immediately by default, but scaling down waits five minutes. That avoids cycles of scale down, spike, scale up. The behavior section lets you tune this.

16
00:01:51,444 --> 00:01:53,454
Watch out for common pitfalls.

17
00:01:53,554 --> 00:01:59,020
Missing CPU requests, even on a sidecar, make the HPA show unknown.

18
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:04,954
Memory-based scaling often never scales down, because many runtimes keep memory after load drops.

19
00:02:05,054 --> 00:02:10,776
Don't keep replicas in your Git manifest when an HPA owns it, or every apply resets the count.

20
00:02:10,876 --> 00:02:17,350
And remember that max replicas also protects downstream systems, like your database's connection limit.

21
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:24,438
KEDA scales on events from outside the cluster, and can scale all the way to zero when there's nothing to do.

22
00:02:24,538 --> 00:02:30,436
Here, a worker scales on the length of an SQS queue, aiming for twenty messages per Pod.

23
00:02:30,536 --> 00:02:40,306
KEDA supports dozens of triggers, including Kafka lag, Prometheus queries and cron schedules. It creates the HPA for you, so don't create your own as well.

24
00:02:41,656 --> 00:02:46,898
The Vertical Pod Autoscaler watches real usage and recommends better requests and limits.

25
00:02:46,998 --> 00:02:53,776
Start with mode Off, which only gives recommendations. The other modes apply them, sometimes by restarting Pods.

26
00:02:53,876 --> 00:03:04,270
Don't let the HPA and VPA both act on CPU for the same workload. A safe combination is the HPA on CPU, and the VPA in recommendation mode.

27
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:08,590
The Cluster Autoscaler works with node groups.

28
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:17,772
When Pods are Pending because nothing fits, it grows a group. When a node's requests stay low for about ten minutes and its Pods can move, it removes the node.

29
00:03:17,872 --> 00:03:26,378
Scale-down can be blocked by strict disruption budgets, local storage, bare Pods, or an annotation that marks Pods as not safe to evict.

30
00:03:27,728 --> 00:03:32,298
Karpenter is a newer node autoscaler. It doesn't use fixed node groups.

31
00:03:32,398 --> 00:03:37,976
It looks at the Pending Pods and launches whatever instance type fits best, within the rules you set.

32
00:03:38,076 --> 00:03:45,398
It's usually faster, it consolidates onto cheaper or fewer nodes, and it can mix spot and on-demand instances.

33
00:03:46,748 --> 00:03:52,646
Scaling isn't instant. If new nodes are needed, a sudden spike can take several minutes to absorb.

34
00:03:52,746 --> 00:04:03,572
To reduce that, keep spare capacity with lower targets, use placeholder Pods that real Pods can push out, make images smaller and startup faster, and scale up before known peaks.

35
00:04:04,922 --> 00:04:06,164
Let's recap.

36
00:04:06,264 --> 00:04:15,058
The HPA scales Pods, KEDA scales on events and to zero, the VPA sizes Pods, and Cluster Autoscaler or Karpenter scale nodes.

37
00:04:15,158 --> 00:04:22,864
Everything depends on resource requests. Remove replicas from Git when an HPA owns it, and plan for the time it takes to scale.

38
00:04:22,964 --> 00:04:27,790
Next, you'll learn who can do what in a cluster, with RBAC and ServiceAccounts.
